      "content": "Welcome to your personalized **Introduction to Python Loops** cheat sheet, designed to help you master iteration in SkillStream Academy. A **for loop** is ideal when you know the number of iterations needed, and it automatically manages the **increment** of the **loop variable** moving through each item in an **iterable** like a list.\n\nHere is a practical example using a standard **conditional** to print numbers:\n```python\nfor i in range(1, 6):\n    if i == 3:\n        continue # Skips to next iteration if condition is met\n    print(f\"Count is: {i}\")\n```\nIn contrast, a **while loop** repeats an action as long as a state remains true, giving you full control over the logic inside its **body**. Use this when the number of iterations depends on a changing **condition** rather than a fixed count, though you must be careful to avoid creating an unintentional **infinite loop**.\n\nWhen working with complex data structures, consider using a **nested loop** to traverse two-dimensional arrays or grids. By understanding the **syntax** differences between these constructs, you can write efficient code that handles **sequences** and **booleans**",
